 Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120023 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120023 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120023 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9c55bfa3-aadc-5e0f-bea4-8915a95c985b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1061 486583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0251d11-c37d-5bc9-839f-3b319b92b170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1061 486584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f33e53a7-67fa-52bd-883d-157e2b1bfbc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1061 486587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34f75092-da67-553a-bcea-fdad4bc5ae09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1061 486590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3987c34c-0de9-5447-a8ac-de5808efdd56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1061 486593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1ff9805-1591-512e-a111-06479708d986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1064 "To determine the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we first cancel any common factors in the numerator and denominator. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFor example, let's try to find the vertical asymptotes of the rational function $y=\\dfrac{2}{x-5}.$ \n\nNow, there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, and so we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =x-5 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a vertical asymptote at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up  with what we see in the graph of the function:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d2c264e323eb86b4e56fef563387989f66fb016a3e1c98db1f35776e3b5cb5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1065 264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "22321890-d586-5e6c-bff9-b2a6b3388ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339890 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52a883e7-4d2a-532d-9bbd-dce6962edcc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339949 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=1$, $x=-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339949 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339949 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a4e1d9b-7c6a-5c7d-b288-86ddd171b3c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340358 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340358 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340358 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "89cc96ba-2754-55f6-8b86-473c4e42f001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340415 #edb/ref 1080 "x=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340415 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340415 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f1ac77d-4b8a-5f0e-932d-f01c276b93ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340674 #edb/ref 1080 "x=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9c87c4a-a6d6-5db3-8002-3e4994f240d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186339890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186339949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186340674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186339890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341637 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "929e3387-fa4c-54fe-ab80-91ba52610238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d13dc12b-246a-57c9-9dc3-693e68fcfaf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341899 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{x+1+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{2x}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=1$ and $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
